AMSAT Satellite Status Page and API
Site: https://www.amsat.org/status
API: https://www.amsat.org/status/api

CHANGE LOG:
2022-07-19: Status page and API shifted to open development. Initial commit to AMSAT Gitlab instance.
2023-04-21: Consolidate config.php references to single config file (in root folder).
2026-03-25: Update color pallete to IBM Colorblind Safe Palette
2026-05-20: Security fixes related to database interactions
2026-05-23:
    - Removed "ISS Crew Active" status option. (Issue #13)
    - Updated status table legend, tool tip, and report types to be in sync
2026-06-17:
    - Migrated repository from GitLab to GitHub
    - CI pipeline ported to GitHub Actions (PHPUnit + Playwright test suites, build artifact)
2026-08-01:
    - Added deploy-time versioning (Issue #20): frontend footer now shows the
      last-deployed date/time and short commit SHA; API responses carry
      X-AMSAT-API-Commit / X-AMSAT-API-Deployed-At headers. Values are
      generated by the CD pipeline at deploy time from github.sha and the
      runner's clock, not stored as a new repo variable.
    - Established the CHANGELOG-update convention for AI agent work,
      documented in docs/agents/changelog-convention.md (not CI-enforced —
      see that doc for why), and refreshed the docs/agents/ knowledge
      bundle, which had gone stale since CD shipped.
    - Commit: ed6fb69
2026-08-09:
    - Report timestamp precision migration (Issue #24). BREAKING CHANGE for
      API consumers of GET/POST /api/v1/reports.php -- no deprecation
      window. If you consume this API, read this entry before upgrading:
        - `satellite` gains two real columns: `observed_at` (when the
          activity happened -- new source of truth) and `submitted_at`
          (when the server received the report; DB-generated only, never
          accepted from request payloads). The old `day`/`hour`/`period`
          columns are frozen as a historical archive -- nothing reads or
          writes them going forward.
        - Fixed a real display bug: `reported_time` in GET/POST responses
          previously always showed `:30:00` regardless of when the report
          actually happened (a `CONCAT(day, "T", LPAD(hour,2,"0"),
          ":30:00Z")` reconstruction bug). It now reflects the real
          submitted time, down to the minute.
        - `period` is removed from the GET /reports.php response. It can
          no longer reflect real data going forward.
        - `replaced_count` is removed from the POST /reports.php response.
          Writes are now non-destructive: submitting another report for
          the same satellite/callsign/time no longer deletes-and-replaces
          the previous one -- every submission is stored. If your
          integration relied on the old delete+insert "correction"
          behavior, you'll need to de-duplicate on your own end (e.g. by
          taking the most recent `reported_time` per satellite/callsign).
        - The `since` filter on GET /reports.php now does a real timestamp
          comparison instead of comparing against the buggy reconstructed
          string above -- boundary behavior may differ slightly from
          before for requests near an exact `:30:00`.
        - Backfilled historical data (everything reported before this
          migration shipped) is only accurate to 15-minute-bucket
          precision -- that's the real precision that existed at capture
          time (the old `period` field), not minute-level. Only reports
          submitted after this shipped have true minute precision. 53 of
          712,980 rows in production had no `period` captured at all (a
          legacy sentinel value) and were backfilled to hour-level
          precision instead of 15-minute precision.
      `frontend/v1/submit.php` now POSTs to the API instead of writing to
      MySQL directly (its first use of the site's own API), and its "you
      already made a report" duplicate-replace warning is gone along with
      the behavior it described. `frontend/v1/index.php`'s status grid,
      tooltips, and "Reports From" list were rewritten against
      `observed_at` in the same PR, since they'd otherwise show nothing
      for any report submitted after this shipped.
      A production DB migration (db/migrations/) targeting dallas190 is
      run manually by the project owner -- separate from this app-code
      deploy, and not wired into CI/CD.
      Added a CI check (tools/check-timezone-pins.php) that fails the
      build if a new raw DB connection is added without the UTC
      time_zone pin every connection needs for TIMESTAMP columns to
      behave correctly -- a regression guard, not a re-verification of
      today's connections (see the script's header comment and the PR
      description for what it does and doesn't catch).